commit ebe3383a3f70a19133cebf954fa36029e37b8be5
Author: Niko1221 <Niko1221@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Tue Oct 27 01:13:20 2026 +0100

    PLE reader: a failed read no longer leaves the next ticket waiting for ever; stress test for the direct table reader (#3602)
    
    Problem. #3602 reports a deterministic all-logits NaN with --ple-io direct on IQ3_S (Windows, two GGUF shards) where
    --ple-io mmap passes; a diagnostic build found non-finite values already in the gathered PLE embedding of one token. The
    bytes the model got were not the file's (the largest finite value, 7.9e6, is what a random fp16 scale gives an IQ4_NL
    row). This commit audits the reader and builds the test the report asks for; it does not claim to have found that
    fault.
    
    Audit (no model run). src/ngram/ple_reader.cpp, src/platform/direct_file.cpp and the consumers (PleTable gather_batch /
    prefetch_rows / collect, prefill's double-buffered ple_gather, verify's windows): rows are copied and cached under the
    reader's mutex after a complete read, short reads are checked per row, straddling rows get a two-page read, the offset
    arithmetic is 64-bit, the set-associative cache and the slot buffers are only touched under the lock, and prefill's two
    host buffers are ordered by the ple_copied events. I found no way to hand out a row before its read completed or from a
    stale buffer.
    
    One real bug, in the error path. After a failed read the I/O worker parks for good (worker_loop), but issue() went on
    queueing jobs, so the next collect() waited for them for ever. issue() now returns a ticket with no jobs once the reader
    has failed, and collect() returns the error at once (and drops the ticket). Same change in the SYCL copy
    (sycl/src/ngram/ple_reader.cpp).
    
    Tests.
    - src/ngram/ple_reader_stress_test.cpp (ctest ple_reader_stress): 7 table shapes (header 0/192/4000/4051, rows of 1, 90,
      110, 160 and 4096 bytes, with and without bytes after the table) x 15 reader configurations (io thread or caller,
      in-flight 1/2/3/64, row cache of 0, 16 and 100000 rows, injected completion delays, keep-alive), four client threads
      with one to three tickets outstanding collected in any order; every delivered row is compared byte for byte with a
      buffered read of the file; collected buffers are compared again later (a late write would show); rows on every page
      phase, the last rows, out-of-range rows; the reader after a failed read (the file is cut short under it); with --big a
      5 GiB sparse table with rows around 2^31 and 2^32 and at its end.
    - table_is_consistent_under_concurrent_use in src/ngram/ple_reader_test.cpp: one PleTable used by a prompt thread
      (big gather_batch), a decode window (prefetch_rows, gather_batch) and single gathers at once, against the mapped reader.
    
    Results (z440, Linux, g++ 13, reader + direct_file + test built alone): plain build with --big OK; ASan+UBSan OK. The
    error-path test against the unchanged reader hangs (the test exits 3 after 20 s); with the change it passes. ThreadSanitizer
    flagged a data race that was a mistake in the test (the delay changed from a second thread in the caller-thread mode,
    which has no lock); fixed in the test.
    
    Not covered. The NaN itself: it needs a run on the reporter's configuration (gate script gates/3602-ple-direct.sh). The
    Windows run of the new test is listed in the stream notes.

